    "text": "Looking at this Java servlet code for SQL injection vulnerabilities.\n\nLet me trace the data flow:\n\n1. User input is obtained: `String param = request.getParameter(\"username\");`\n2. SQL query is constructed with direct concatenation: `String sql = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '\" + param + \"'\";`\n3. The query is executed using a regular Statement: `stmt.executeQuery(sql);`\n\nThe code takes untrusted user input from the HTTP request parameter and directly concatenates it into a SQL query string without any parameterization or sanitization. This creates a classic SQL injection vulnerability where an attacker could inject malicious SQL code.\n\n```verdict\nvulnerable: true\ncwe: 89\nreasoning: User input from request.getParameter(\"username\") is directly concatenated into the SQL query using string concatenation, then executed with Statement.executeQuery(). This allows attackers to inject arbitrary SQL code by manipulating the username parameter.\n```",
